===============================================================================

ApprovalEngine installed.

Next steps:

  1. Run the migrations:

       rails db:migrate

  2. Edit config/initializers/approval_engine.rb and set BOTH:
       * actor_class           — your approver class (e.g. "User")
       * current_tenant_method — REQUIRED. Until it's set, auto-routing on
                                 create silently does nothing. Single-tenant
                                 apps can return a constant.

  3. Make your actor class resolve approval groups:

       class User < ApplicationRecord
         def self.resolve_approval_group(group_name, target)
           where(role: group_name)
         end
       end

  4. Arm a model and declare its safe surface (a rule can only read attributes
     you expose here):

       class Invoice < ApplicationRecord
         has_approvals

         exposes_for_approval do
           attribute :amount, type: :decimal
         end

         def after_approved
           PaymentService.disburse_funds!(self)
         end
       end

  5. Define the flow in db/seeds.rb — one block builds the template, its steps
     and the rule that routes to it. Re-running it reconciles the same rows, so
     it is safe on every deploy:

       ApprovalEngine.define_flow "High-value invoice", tenant: account.id, model: Invoice do
         on :create, when: { amount: { gt: 10_000 } }
         step "Manager", group: "manager"
         step "CFO",     group: "cfo"
       end

     Then CONFIRM the wiring before relying on it:

       invoice.preview_approval(event: "invoice.created").triggered?
       # false? -> check event_name match, the template's `active` status,
       #           the rule condition, and that its vars are exposed attributes.

  6. (Optional) Copy the approval views to customise them:

       rails generate approval_engine:views

  7. (Optional) Mount the ops dashboard in config/routes.rb:

       mount ApprovalEngine::Engine => "/approval_engine"

  8. (Optional) Turn on the built-in notifications. They are OFF by default so
     that installing or upgrading the gem never mails your users on its own.
     Enabling them needs THREE things, not one:

       config.notifications_enabled = true
       config.mailer_from           = "approvals@example.com"  # or parent_mailer
       config.approval_url_builder  = ->(a) { invoice_url(a.target) }

     Without a From address nothing can be delivered; without a URL builder the
     mail arrives with nowhere to go. Set config.approval_recipients too, or the
     approved / rejected / changes-requested emails stay silent — the engine
     knows who approves, but only you know who asked.

  9. (Optional) Schedule the reminder sweep so a quiet approver gets nudged.
     Set the threshold, then run the job on whatever recurring mechanism you
     already have (solid_queue recurring tasks, sidekiq-cron, whenever, cron):

       config.reminder_after = 2.days.to_i

       ApprovalEngine::ReminderSweepJob.perform_later              # all tenants
       ApprovalEngine::ReminderSweepJob.perform_later(tenant_id: account.id)

     Each step is nudged at most once, so running it often is safe. While
     reminder_after is nil the sweep is a no-op.

